  "solution": {
    "notes": [
      "The cumulative balance determines how many dresses must cross each boundary between adjacent machines.",
      "A positive local surplus can require all surplus dresses to leave a machine in separate simultaneous transfers, so local surplus must also be considered.",
      "The maximum of the local positive surplus and absolute prefix imbalance is both a lower bound and achievable under the simultaneous-transfer rules.",
      "Use integer arithmetic throughout; the total number of dresses is at most 10^9."
    ],
    "approach": "First compute the total number of dresses. If it is not divisible by the number of machines, return -1. Otherwise let target be the average. Scan from left to right, maintaining balance, the net surplus of the processed prefix relative to its target amount. For each machine, update balance by machines[i] - target and maximize the answer with both the machine's positive surplus and abs(balance). This maximum is exactly the minimum number of moves.",
    "editorial": {
      "better": {
        "complexity": "O(n) time and O(1) extra space.",
        "description": "Avoid simulating individual moves. After checking divisibility, compute the target average and use prefix conservation: the cumulative imbalance gives the unavoidable flow across each boundary, while each machine's positive surplus gives the other necessary lower bound. This prefix-flow characterization is already the asymptotically optimal approach."
      },
      "optimal": {
        "complexity": "O(n) time and O(1) extra space.",
        "description": "Let target be the total dresses divided by n. During one left-to-right scan, set delta = machines[i] - target and add it to balance. abs(balance) is the number of dresses that must cross the boundary after i, while positive delta is the number that must leave the current machine. The maximum of these values over all positions is the exact minimum number of moves."
      },
      "bruteForce": {
        "complexity": "Exponential in the worst case.",
        "description": "Enumerate possible simultaneous transfers and resulting machine configurations until every machine is equal. This explores a rapidly growing state space and is practical only for tiny inputs."
      }
    },
    "timeComplexity": "O(n)",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1)",
    "code": {
      "C": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint findMinMoves(int* machines, int machinesSize) {\n    long long total = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < machinesSize; ++i) total += machines[i];\n    if (total % machinesSize != 0) return -1;\n    long long target = total / machinesSize;\n    long long balance = 0, answer = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < machinesSize; ++i) {\n        long long delta = (long long)machines[i] - target;\n        balance += delta;\n        long long absBalance = balance < 0 ? -balance : balance;\n        if (delta > answer) answer = delta;\n        if (absBalance > answer) answer = absBalance;\n    }\n    return (int)answer;\n}",
      "C++": "class Solution {\npublic:\n    int findMinMoves(vector<int>& machines) {\n        long long total = 0;\n        for (int x : machines) total += x;\n        int n = (int)machines.size();\n        if (total % n != 0) return -1;\n        long long target = total / n;\n        long long balance = 0, answer = 0;\n        for (int x : machines) {\n            long long delta = (long long)x - target;\n            balance += delta;\n            answer = max(answer, max(delta, llabs(balance)));\n        }\n        return (int)answer;\n    }\n};",
      "Java": "class Solution {\n    public int findMinMoves(int[] machines) {\n        long total = 0;\n        for (int x : machines) total += x;\n        int n = machines.length;\n        if (total % n != 0) return -1;\n        long target = total / n;\n        long balance = 0, answer = 0;\n        for (int x : machines) {\n            long delta = x - target;\n            balance += delta;\n            answer = Math.max(answer, Math.max(delta, Math.abs(balance)));\n        }\n        return (int) answer;\n    }\n}",
      "Python3": "import sys, json\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def findMinMoves(self, machines: List[int]) -> int:\n        total = sum(machines)\n        n = len(machines)\n        if total % n != 0:\n            return -1\n        target = total // n\n        balance = 0\n        answer = 0\n        for dresses in machines:\n            delta = dresses - target\n            balance += delta\n            answer = max(answer, delta, abs(balance))\n        return answer",
      "JavaScript": "const readline = require('readline');\nfunction findMinMoves(machines) {\n    const total = machines.reduce((a,b) => a+b, 0);\n    const n = machines.length;\n    if (total % n !== 0) return -1;\n    const target = total / n;\n    let balance = 0, answer = 0;\n    for (const dresses of machines) {\n        const delta = dresses - target;\n        balance += delta;\n        answer = Math.max(answer, delta, Math.abs(balance));\n    }\n    return answer;\n}"
    }
